  "completion": "<think>\nAlright, let's tackle this forecast. The user is asking about the probability that an AI will contribute to solving one of the Millennium Prize Problems by various deadlines. First, I need to list the problems: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, Yang-Mills. Poincar\u00e9 is already solved, so it's excluded.\n\nThe key here is that the solution needs to be a correct, complete one, and the AI must have contributed a new mathematical argument or proof step. Also, the solution has to be publicly available. Partial progress doesn't count. So the question is when an AI might make that kind of contribution.\n\nCurrent AI capabilities: The latest models like GPT-4, Gemini, Claude but they're good at specific tasks but not for proving complex theorems. However, future AIs might be more capable. The user mentions considering systems more capable than myself, so I'm to assume hypothetical AI that's better.\n\nHow long until such an AI exists? If we look at trends in AI development, maybe late 2030s to 2040s? But it might depend on breakthroughs in AI systems that can understand and create mathematical proofs.\n\nFor the deadlines, 2026 is soon. Given the current state, the probability is low, maybe 1-2%. By 2030, maybe 5%? As of 2035, perhaps 10-15%, 2040 maybe 20-30%, and 2050 maybe 50%? But I should be cautious because solving these problems is hard. Navier-Stokes might be more approachable with computational methods which AI could assist in. However, proving P vs NP is very hard, but an AI could find a key insight.\n\nBut maybe some problems can be solved without AI. The event is that an AI contributes a necessary step. Non-identifiable contributions don't count. Human-AI collaboration is allowed. So if an AI suggests a step but humans do the proof, that might qualify. But the AI's contribution must be identifiable and necessary.\n\nWait, the user said \"substantive human-AI collaboration qualifies.\" So even if it's